---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Corrections">
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-inner">
      <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Corrections · The registry's own ledger</div>
      <h1>What we got <em>wrong</em>, and when.</h1>
      <p class="page-hero-lede">
        Every substantive change to a published case is logged below, in reverse chronological order. To request a correction or exercise your right of response, use the intake form first. Typos and stylistic edits are not logged.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="prose" id="submit">
    <h2>Request a correction or right of response</h2>
    <p>If you are named in a case, or you see something the registry got wrong, use this form. A human reads every submission and replies within five business days. Substantive changes are logged below with the date and the source that prompted them.</p>
    <form class="corrections-form" action="https://api.web3forms.com/submit" method="POST">
      <input type="hidden" name="access_key" value="8d276028-ab5b-4f91-884b-463900267306" />
      <input type="hidden" name="subject" value="Correction request — wacountability.org" />
      <input type="hidden" name="from_name" value="WA Accountability Registry — corrections form" />
      <input type="hidden" name="redirect" value="https://wacountability.org/corrections/thanks" />
      <div class="corrections-form-row">
        <label for="cf-name">Your name <span class="corrections-form-optional">(optional, but helpful)</span></label>
        <input id="cf-name" name="name" type="text" autocomplete="name" />
      </div>
      <div class="corrections-form-row">
        <label for="cf-email">Email <span class="corrections-form-required">(required so we can reply)</span></label>
        <input id="cf-email" name="email" type="email" autocomplete="email" required />
      </div>
      <div class="corrections-form-row">
        <label for="cf-affiliation">Affiliation <span class="corrections-form-optional">(if relevant — agency, firm, organization)</span></label>
        <input id="cf-affiliation" name="affiliation" type="text" />
      </div>
      <div class="corrections-form-row">
        <label for="cf-case-id">Case ID or slug <span class="corrections-form-optional">(e.g. KC-2026-001 or /cases/dchs-systemic-audit)</span></label>
        <input id="cf-case-id" name="case_id" type="text" />
      </div>
      <div class="corrections-form-row">
        <label for="cf-type">Type of submission</label>
        <select id="cf-type" name="submission_type" required>
          <option value="correction">Correction — a fact in the record is wrong</option>
          <option value="right_of_response">Right of response — I'm named and want my position on the record</option>
          <option value="new_source">New source — adds to or updates a case</option>
          <option value="new_case">New case — something the registry should be tracking</option>
          <option value="other">Other</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div class="corrections-form-row">
        <label for="cf-detail">Detail <span class="corrections-form-required">(required)</span></label>
        <textarea id="cf-detail" name="detail" rows="8" required placeholder="Quote the specific sentence or field you dispute, explain what's wrong, and link to a document or source that supports your position."></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="corrections-form-row">
        <label for="cf-sources">Supporting links or document URLs <span class="corrections-form-optional">(one per line)</span></label>
        <textarea id="cf-sources" name="sources" rows="3" placeholder="https://..."></textarea>
      </div>
      <!-- Honeypot for bots; humans leave it empty (Web3Forms reserved field name) -->
      <div class="corrections-form-honeypot" aria-hidden="true">
        <label for="cf-website">Website</label>
        <input id="cf-website" name="botcheck" type="checkbox" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
      </div>
      <div class="corrections-form-actions">
        <button type="submit" class="corrections-form-submit">Send to the registry</button>
        <p class="corrections-form-fineprint">Your email is used only to reply. The registry does not publish your name or email unless you explicitly ask us to add an on-the-record response to a case.</p>
      </div>
    </form>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Policy</h2>
    <p>A registry that calls out other people's bookkeeping has to show its own. When a published case is materially changed — a dollar figure corrected, a name added or removed, a legal-status field updated, an evidentiary tier downgraded — the change appears below with the date, the case slug, the field changed, and the source that prompted it. Old URLs remain live; pages are amended, not deleted. Cases that are formally exonerated are tombstoned at the same URL with an exoneration note.</p>

    <h2>2026-10-20 · Former-draft re-verification</h2>
    <p>The 17 records published from draft status earlier today were re-checked against their primary sources.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/cases/oco-bourgeois-firing">WA-2026-OCO-BOURGEOIS-FIRING</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The record said the statute sets no grounds for removing the corrections ombuds. RCW 43.06C.030(2) allows removal "only for neglect of duty, misconduct, or the inability to perform duties" (<a href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/RCW/default.aspx?cite=43.06C.030">RCW 43.06C.030</a>). The title implied a link to the federal Civil Rights of Institutionalized Persons Act (CRIPA) investigation that no source supports; it was retitled. A Reddit source and an unsupported civil-rights-harm severity tag were removed. Body rewritten with governor's office and Bourgeois accounts attributed.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/doc-oco-solitary-confinement">WA-2024-DOC-SOLITARY</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The title said suicide in solitary was "33x the national average" and attributed that to the Corrections Ombuds. The Ombuds reports make no such comparison. The figure came from a Prison Legal News calculation (467 per 100,000) that divides 14 deaths over ten years by a population count and sets it against a one-year national rate, which is not a valid comparison. The record also wrongly said HB 1090 set a 90% reduction mandate (the 90% goal is DOC's own 2023 pledge; the reports were requested by an ESSB 5187 budget proviso), presented "one-eighth of the funding" as a legislative finding (it is a DOC deputy secretary's description of DOC's own request), and said the director removed in 2026 led the office during the 2024 reports (he was appointed in late 2025). Title, summary, reform argument and body rewritten.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/dcyf-ofco-critical-incident-multiyear">WA-2024-DCYF-OFCO-FATALITIES</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The title and lede compared OFCO's 92 first-half-2025 incidents with "36 reported by DCYF." No source supports a DCYF count of 36; it appears to have been confused with a 36% neglect figure. DCYF's archived July 2025 briefing reports 22 DCYF-reviewable incidents for Q1 2025 under narrower statutory criteria, against OFCO's 47 (<a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20260316211009/https://dcyf.wa.gov/sites/default/files/pdf/CriticalIncidentsQ12025_Briefing%20Slides_DCYFsResponse_July%2029%2C2025.pdf">DCYF briefing, archived</a>). Unsupported claims that the July 2025 disclosure was unprecedented were also removed.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/one-washington-erp-risks">WA-2024-ONE-WASHINGTON</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The record said "the July 2025 go-live proceeded." It did not; the launch was postponed in late 2024 and is now set for October 2027 to October 2028 (<a href="https://www.govtech.com/gov-experience/two-washington-state-it-modernization-efforts-get-new-life">GovTech</a>). It also described $465 million as the full program cost; the State Auditor's figure covers only the Phase 1A core-financials work, and the full program is now estimated at $776 million. Title softened from "lacks contingency plan" to the auditor's "not yet finalized."</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/dshs-scc-brown-water-settlement">WA-2024-DSHS-SCC-WATER</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The record said $7.325 million went to residents after $2.5 million in attorney fees from a $7.5 million settlement, which does not add up. The News Tribune reported $7.5 million; The Center Square later called it a $7.325 million judgment. Both are now attributed, and the headline figure is the contemporaneous $7.5 million.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/doh-hospital-oversight-jlarc">WA-2025-DOH-HOSPITAL-JLARC</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The title said mandatory adverse-event reviews had not been done "since 2011." The JLARC (Joint Legislative Audit and Review Committee) report says DOH does not review these reports but gives no start date; it also says DOH's 2016 and 2020 funding requests went unfunded, which contradicts the record's claim that this was "not a resource gap." Status updated for DOH's revised plan presented July 15, 2026 (<a href="https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/WALEG/bulletins/420f474">JLARC bulletin</a>).</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/dshs-hha-adult-family-homes-hhs-oig">WA-2024-DSHS-HHA-OIG</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The report number was wrong (OEI-09-23-00370); the HHS Office of Inspector General report is A-09-23-02002. The 17-of-20 and 214-instance figures were confirmed.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/dol-license-express-backdoor">WA-2025-DOL-LICENSE-EXPRESS</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The title and lede stated the tort claim's allegations (1,000+ identity thefts, prior WSP warnings, a budget-based decision not to fix) as fact. They are now attributed to the claim, and DOL's denial is in the lede. <code>evidentiary_status</code> changed from documented to alleged. A FOX 13 article was mislabeled as a tier-1 agency statement; it is now tier 2. October 2026 coverage added.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/ospi-nd-reykdal-idea-settlement">WA-2024-OSPI-IDEA</a>: Updated: final approval granted June 23, 2025, with $416,915 in attorney fees (<a href="https://ospi.k12.wa.us/sites/default/files/2025-06/ndvreykdalfinalorder.pdf">final order</a>). <strong>Correction:</strong> the lede said the settlement found OSPI violated federal law; settlements make no such finding. It now cites the Ninth Circuit's preliminary-injunction ruling instead.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/dshs-dda-ombuds-failures">WA-2024-DSHS-DDA-OMBUDS</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The DD Ombuds report describes the settlement as "reportedly 9 million dollars" over alleged abuse and neglect. The record stated it as a documented $9M payment by the provider "following documented abuse." Wording now matches the source.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/state-agency-tort-payouts-aggregate">WA-2025-TORT-AGGREGATE</a>: Updated: HB 2706 and SB 5144 died in committee; E2SSB 6239 passed the Senate 33-16 but got no House hearing. <code>reform_status</code> changed from bill_in_committee to bill_died.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/wsp-records-destruction-settlement">WA-2026-WSP-RECORDS-DESTRUCTION</a>: Removed an unsourced sentence implying the February 2025 retention schedule was timed to public scrutiny. Status note added.</li>
      <li>Re-checked against primary sources with no substantive change other than verification dates: <a href="/cases/dcyf-ofco-annual-findings">WA-2024-DCYF-OFCO-FINDINGS</a>, <a href="/cases/doc-oco-unexpected-fatality-review">WA-2024-DOC-UFR</a>, <a href="/cases/dcyf-oakley-carlson-fatality-review">WA-2024-DCYF-OAKLEY-CARLSON</a>, <a href="/cases/doc-wccw-use-of-force">WA-2025-DOC-WCCW-FORCE</a>, <a href="/cases/cjtc-deescalation-training-noncompliance">WA-2026-CJTC-TRAINING</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2026-10-20 · Draft records published; ombuds records merged</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>19 records changed from <code>draft</code> to <code>published</code>.</strong> These pages were already publicly visible, because the site did not filter draft records. The label change does not by itself mean each record was re-verified; re-verification is tracked by each record's <code>last_verified</code> date.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/leb-25-09-jinkins-public-resources">WA-2025-009 — LEB 25-09 (Jinkins)</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The record said a complaint was filed October 27, 2025 and no opinion had been issued. In fact the complaint was received March 20, 2025, and on October 27, 2025 the Board found reasonable cause to believe Speaker Jinkins and three House staff violated RCW 42.52.160 and .180. It imposed no penalty (<a href="https://leg.wa.gov/media/h5glcmzw/ethicscom202509.pdf">LEB order</a>). Title, actors (placeholder replaced with the three named staff), <code>evidentiary_status</code> (alleged → documented), <code>outcome_summary</code>, and body rewritten.</li>
      <li><strong>KC-2025-003 merged into <a href="/cases/kc-ombuds-dchs-forensic-clark-nuber">KC-2026-008</a>.</strong> The two records covered the same investigation; the old URL redirects. During the merge, three examples in KC-2025-003 (a payment 46% above budget, suspected forged invoices, a $111,000 donation) were dropped because the record cited no locatable source for them. The $14,000 loan example is retained with its <a href="https://www.seattletimes.com/seattle-news/politics/king-county-fraud-investigation-shifts-to-independent-office/">Seattle Times</a> source. Cross-references in KC-2025-001 and the inspector general record were updated.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2026-10-20 · October status updates, batch 2</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/cases/leb-enforcement-pattern-meta">WA-2026-META-LEB</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The page said the status of SB 5143 was unverified and described the Ethics Act as unchanged since 1994. SB 5143 was enacted as Chapter 377, Laws of 2025. It removed the combined $5,000 cap on penalties plus costs and raised the gift threshold to $100, while keeping the $5,000 per-violation cap (<a href="https://lawfilesext.leg.wa.gov/biennium/2025-26/Pdf/Bill%20Reports/Senate/5143-S.E%20SBR%20FBR%2025.pdf">Final Bill Report</a>). <code>reform_status</code> changed from <code>bill_introduced</code> to <code>bill_passed</code>; <code>remediation_note</code>, <code>reform_status_note</code>, title and body updated. The hearing count, previously "~2 public hearings in 32 years" while listing three, now reads "two public hearings in a decade," which is what the cited source supports.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/leb-25-05-simmons-conflict-special-privileges">WA-2025-005 — LEB 25-05</a>: <code>reform_argument</code> clarified. The costs were recoverable in full because of SB 5143's 2025 change.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/kc-dchs-ombuds-investigation">KC-2025-003</a> and <a href="/cases/kc-ombuds-dchs-forensic-clark-nuber">KC-2026-008</a>: <code>reform_status</code> changed to <code>structural_change_implemented</code>. Ordinance 2026-0101 creating an Inspector General Division passed July 7, 2026 (<a href="https://mkcclegisearch.kingcounty.gov/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=7991470&amp;GUID=4F5FD59F-DB76-4B00-A0AE-9B133F28ED01&amp;Options=Advanced&amp;Search=">Ordinance 20101</a>). KC-2025-003 <strong>correction</strong>: the record stated "approximately $320,000" as the net improperly disbursed amount. That is DCHS's figure; Clark Nuber's is $446,703, and the Ombuds declined DCHS's requested change (<a href="https://cdn.kingcounty.gov/-/media/king-county/independent/governance-and-leadership/government-oversight/office-of-the-ombuds/reports/dchs-programmatic-contract-evaluation-omb20250697.pdf?rev=fe8aae2694564a26b80b28ee23a160e4&amp;hash=47C130F3291B4CA772BF9A75A411B337">Ombuds report</a>). Both figures are now attributed. The report release date was corrected from May 20 to May 19, 2026.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/seattle-city-auditor-recommendation-implementation-status">Seattle City Auditor recommendation status</a>: <code>outcome_summary</code> updated for the May 27, 2026 report; <code>next_milestone</code> moved to the 2027 cycle.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/doj-cripa-wccw">DOJ CRIPA — WCCW</a>: status update only (no findings issued).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2026-10-20 · October status updates and corrections</h2>
    <p>Six stale case files were re-verified against primary sources and updated. Material changes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/cases/leb-25-38-simmons-special-privileges">WA-2026-038 — LEB 25-38 (Simmons)</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> This record previously described 25-38 as concerning $30,000 in surplus campaign-fund donations, citing InvestigateWest. That was wrong. Per the <a href="https://leg.wa.gov/about-the-legislature/ethics/ethics-complaint-opinions/25-38/">LEB opinion</a>, 25-38 concerned a post on Simmons' personal Facebook page and was dismissed for lack of jurisdiction. Title, <code>outcome_summary</code>, <code>reform_argument</code>, tags and body rewritten; the InvestigateWest source was removed from this record.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/leb-25-05-simmons-conflict-special-privileges">WA-2025-005 — LEB 25-05 (Simmons)</a>: <strong>new record.</strong> Adjudicated: three violations, $15,000 civil penalty plus $37,457.98 in costs, per the <a href="https://leg.wa.gov/media/jdzoy2qi/ethicscom202505.pdf">August 28, 2026 final order</a>. <code>legal_status</code> = <code>civil_filed</code> reflects the respondent's reported petition for judicial review in Thurston County Superior Court.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/leb-enforcement-pattern-meta">WA-2026-META-LEB</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The June 2026 Simmons hearing was mislabeled as case 25-38; it was 25-05. Penalty statistics updated to include the Simmons order; <code>next_milestone</code> changed.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/kcrha-forensic-audit">KCRHA forensic audit</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The record said Mayor Katie Wilson called for KCRHA's dissolution. Her April 22, 2026 statement was "All options are on the table" (<a href="https://westseattleblog.com/2026/04/the-agency-is-unable-to-account-for-13-million-in-public-funds-what-the-mayor-says-about-regional-homelessness-agency-review-results/">West Seattle Blog</a>); her July 1 plan kept the agency. Also updated: <code>outcome_summary</code> (July restructuring; $16.7M unresolved funding need per <a href="https://kcrha.org/news-kcrha-financial-update-external-review-findings/">KCRHA, Oct 15, 2026</a>), remediation and reform notes, CAP date corrected to May 22, <code>next_milestone</code> to January 2027.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/sound-transit-st3-program-reset">Sound Transit ST3 program reset</a>: <code>reform_status</code> changed from <code>bill_in_committee</code> to <code>bill_died</code> (SB 6148 returned to Senate Rules March 12, 2026, per the <a href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/billsummary/?BillNumber=6148&amp;Year=2026">Legislature</a>). <code>outcome_summary</code> updated for the May 28, 2026 Board adoption of <a href="https://www.soundtransit.org/st_sharepoint/download/sites/PRDA/FinalRecords/2026/Resolution%20R2026-11.pdf">Resolution R2026-11</a>.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/kc-rcp-accountability-gap">King County RCP accountability gap</a>: <code>reform_status</code> changed from <code>rulemaking_in_progress</code> to <code>structural_change_implemented</code>. The adopted 2026-27 budget (Ordinance 20023) replaced RCP with KCYDI, per the <a href="https://aqua.kingcounty.gov/council/agendas/LJ/20260701-LJ-Packet.pdf">July 1, 2026 Council staff report</a>.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/ospi-school-funding-it-system">OSPI school-funding IT system</a>: <strong>correction.</strong> The record said OSPI had not announced a modernization timeline. OSPI's response in the <a href="https://portal.sao.wa.gov/ReportSearch/Home/ViewReportFile?arn=1039634&amp;isFinding=false&amp;sp=false">SAO report</a> gave one (development from July 2026, implementation 2028-29). <code>outcome_summary</code> added.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/commerce-digital-navigator-audit">Commerce Digital Navigator audit</a>: <code>review_status</code> changed from <code>draft</code> to <code>published</code>, with an October status update. (Correction to this entry: the page was already publicly visible before this change, because the site does not filter draft records. An earlier version of this entry called it a first publication.) <code>remediation_status</code> set to <code>in_progress</code>: Commerce's own schedule in <a href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/ReportsToTheLegislature/Home/GetPDF?fileName=Department%20of%20Commerce_fa195406-b589-4350-8cc8-d69d8a5eebb3.pdf">SAO Report 1039826</a> lists the findings as partially corrected. Cross-reference added to LEB 25-05, which involves the same organization (Equity in Education Center) and a different funding stream.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/sound-transit-west-seattle-cost">Sound Transit West Seattle Link</a>: <code>outcome_summary</code> updated (Avalon Station dropped in the May 28 plan; opening reported as 2035).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2026-05-20 · KCRHA title language</h2>
    <p><a href="/cases/kcrha-forensic-audit">KCRHA forensic audit</a>: removed the phrase "no fraud found" from the case title and <code>outcome_summary</code> field. The audit is active and ongoing, and a corrective-action plan was not yet due at the time of release. Stating "no fraud found" at the headline level reads as a conclusion when the matter is in fact under investigation. The audit's own statement that it did not find fraud is preserved in the case body, alongside the audit's caveat identifying gift cards, purchase cards, employee reimbursements, and account permissions as four high-risk areas where fraud could occur without being detected under existing controls.</p>

    <h2>2026-05-20 · SPS batch intake (4 new cases)</h2>
    <p>Four new Seattle Public Schools cases added through the INTAKE.md workflow. All passed §1 hard-reject gates and §4 inline cross-reference checks. The new cases:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/cases/sps-garfield-sex-abuse-settlement">SPS-2024-001 — Garfield High School coach sexual abuse, $16M settlement</a>. Tier 1: PWRFL Law settlement announcement; Walter Junior Jones mistrial December 2025 (King County, Judge Michael Ryan); Marvin Wayne Hall guilty plea summer 2025. Tier 2: <a href="https://www.seattletimes.com/education-lab/seattle-public-schools-to-pay-16m-to-settle-sex-abuse-lawsuit/">Seattle Times</a>, <a href="https://www.fox13seattle.com/news/sps-sexual-abuse-tort-claim">Fox 13 / Wixey</a>, <a href="https://www.kiro7.com/news/local/mistrial-declared-child-rape-case-involving-coach-with-seattle-public-schools/DYLQHK5M5ZEXRDHSPCJPTZ2FAM/">KIRO 7</a>.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/sps-garfield-hazing-first-amendment-settlement">SPS-2024-002 — Garfield swim team hazing / First Amendment retaliation, $125K settlement</a>. Tier 1: King County Superior Court Case No. 22-2-10907-6 SEA; <a href="https://law.justia.com/cases/washington/court-of-appeals-division-i/2024/85998-6.html">Washington Court of Appeals parallel PRA case (No. 85998-6-I)</a>. Tier 2: <a href="https://www.kiro7.com/news/local/former-seattle-student-wins-settlement-against-sps/JERFW36DTBCAVKYDHCHVYKZ7CI/">KIRO 7</a>.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/sps-nathan-hale-antisemitism-lawsuit">SPS-2025-004 — Nathan Hale High School antisemitism lawsuit (state + federal)</a>. Tier 1: SPS district statement (Sophia Charcuk, June 17, 2025); federal complaint filed November 20, 2025 under Title VI. Tier 2: <a href="https://www.seattletimes.com/education-lab/suit-alleges-antisemitic-bullying-at-seattles-nathan-hale-high/">Seattle Times / Bryan</a>, <a href="https://katu.com/news/local/nathan-hale-high-seattle-public-schools-lawsuit-former-student-jewish-antisemitism-harassment-verbal-assaults">KATU-KOMO / Kent</a>, <a href="https://www.kiro7.com/news/local/family-says-seattle-public-schools-ignored-threats-slurs-swastikas-targeting-jewish-student/BT4KN32Q4JEPPKIUOIS5F6RRWI/">KIRO 7 / Garrett</a>.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/sps-ag-pregnancy-lactation-lawsuit">SPS-2025-005 — WA AG civil rights lawsuit over pregnancy and lactation accommodations</a>. Tier 1: <a href="https://www.atg.wa.gov/news/news-releases/attorney-general-s-office-sues-seattle-public-schools-over-illegal-treatment">WA AG press release (April 15, 2025)</a>; SPS Policy 5010SP.B (November 2025). Tier 2: <a href="https://www.seattletimes.com/education-lab/wa-sues-seattle-schools-over-its-treatment-of-pregnant-and-nursing-employees/">Seattle Times / Bryan</a>, KIRO 7, KOMO News, Fox 13.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Rejected during triage (failed §1 G3 source-tier gate): toxic-work-environment Instagram post, remote-work salary critique (Instagram), former-employees Facebook lawsuit reference, and unsourced pattern-claim items. The four published cases all pass the documented standard with multiple Tier 1 and Tier 2 sources.</p>

    <h2>2026-05-20 · Cross-case consistency pass</h2>
    <p>A structural pass across all 36 cases checking for broken cross-references, date conflicts, dollar-baseline confusion, inconsistent actor metadata, and tag duplicates. Twenty issues identified; five high/medium-severity fixes applied below. Low-severity tag normalizations folded in.</p>

    <h3>Broken cross-case references</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/cases/port-seattle-pie-fraud">Port of Seattle PIE fraud</a>: Cross-reference to KCRHA forensic audit corrected from <code>KC-2025-007</code> to <code>KC-2026-001</code>.</li>
      <li><a href="/cases/kc-youth-program-self-dealing">KC youth program self-dealing</a>: Cross-reference to King County Inspector General proposal corrected from <code>KC-2026-002</code> to <code>KC-2026-007</code>.</li>
    </ul>

